          "content": "Unit 3 explored computer-intensive non-parametric resampling methods, focusing on the Jackknife and the Bootstrap. We derived Tukey's jackknife bias correction and pseudo-value variance estimation, analyzing its failure on non-smooth functionals such as the sample median. We formulated Bradley Efron's non-parametric bootstrap, establishing that drawing with replacement from the empirical distribution function $F_n$ approximates sampling from the true population distribution $F$. Using Edgeworth expansions, we demonstrated that while percentile and normal-approximation bootstrap intervals achieve only first-order accuracy with $O(n^{-1/2})$ coverage error, studentized bootstrap-t and bias-corrected accelerated ($BC_a$) intervals achieve second-order accuracy with $O(n^{-1})$ coverage error. For dependent time series, we introduced the Moving Block Bootstrap and the Politis-Romano Stationary Bootstrap with geometric block lengths to preserve auto-covariance structures. Finally, we demonstrated how the $m$-out-of-$n$ bootstrap and subsampling resolve non-regular estimation problems where the standard bootstrap fails.",
