          "secNumber": "4.16",
          "heading": "Unit 4 Review and Methodological Synthesis",
          "content": "Unit 4 established the theoretical framework of Monte Carlo integration and its asymptotic convergence properties. We proved that Crude Monte Carlo integration achieves an error rate of $\\sigma / \\sqrt{n}$, rendering its convergence rate entirely independent of spatial dimension $d$ and overcoming the curse of dimensionality that cripples deterministic quadrature grids. We formulated Quasi-Monte Carlo (QMC) integration using low-discrepancy point sets (Halton, Sobol', and Faure sequences), proving via the Koksma-Hlawka inequality that deterministic integration error scales as $O((\\log n)^d / n)$. We resolved the apparent paradox of high-dimensional QMC performance through ANOVA functional decompositions, demonstrating that models dominated by low truncation or superposition dimensions integrate with near-linear speed. We analyzed Randomized Quasi-Monte Carlo (RQMC) via Cranley-Patterson rotations and Owen's scrambled nets, which combine QMC speed with valid statistical standard error computation. Finally, we surveyed Giles' Multilevel Monte Carlo framework, which decouples time-discretization errors in stochastic differential equations.",
